{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>After the death.</b> Rashi's comment: what need is there for Scripture to say this, etc. — that is, what does the verse <b>וידבר HaShem אל משה אחרי מות</b> come to tell us, when it is already written after it: <b>ויאמר HaShem אל משה דבר אל אהרן</b> etc.? Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah compared it to a parable, etc. — this one urged him more than the first, etc. — that is, for in the second verse it is written <b>ולא ימות</b>, which is not written in the first verse, and this is what Rashi explained: therefore it says 'after the death' and <b>ואל יבא בכל עת</b> etc. — that is, because the first verse did not warn him adequately, he came back and wrote you a second verse to warn him more. And thus it is in Torat Kohanim: <b>ויאמר HaShem אל משה דבר אל אהרן אחיך ואל יבא</b> etc. — and we do not know what was said to him in the first utterance. Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah compared it, etc. — this one urged him more than the first; therefore it says 'after the death of the two sons of Aaron.'"], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {0 "The printed text reads פרש״י for Rashi and uses reverent abbreviations (ה׳, ר׳); these are expanded mechanically in translation. The phrase מה ת״ל וכו׳ and וכו׳‎ are rendered as 'what need is there for Scripture to say this, etc.' following the compiler's abbreviated citation convention."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:12:41.210295467Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}